CARD 1 "Theorem 6.12: Properties of the Integral — Linearity & Order"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.12 (Properties of the Integral) — Statement & Proof of (a):</strong><br>Enunciar las 5 propiedades (a-e) y demostrar la parte (a) (linealidad en el integrando):<br><ul><li>(a) $f_1, f_2 \in \mathscr R(\alpha) \implies f_1 + f_2 \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ y $\int (f_1+f_2)d\alpha = \int f_1 d\alpha + \int f_2 d\alpha$; $cf \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ y $\int cf d\alpha = c \int f d\alpha$.</li><li>(b) $f_1 \le f_2 \implies \int f_1 d\alpha \le \int f_2 d\alpha$.</li><li>(c) Aditividad en dominios $[a,c]$ y $[c,b]$.</li><li>(d) $|f| \le M \implies |\int_a^b f d\alpha| \le M[\alpha(b)-\alpha(a)]$.</li><li>(e) Linealidad en el integrador $\alpha$.</li></ul>
BACK
<strong>Demostración de (a):</strong><br>For $f = f_1 + f_2$ and any partition $P$:<br>$$L(P,f_1,\alpha) + L(P,f_2,\alpha) \le L(P,f,\alpha) \le U(P,f,\alpha) \le U(P,f_1,\alpha) + U(P,f_2,\alpha)$$<br>Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, choose $P_1, P_2$ such that $U(P_j,f_j,\alpha) - L(P_j,f_j,\alpha) \lt  \varepsilon$ ($j=1,2$). Replacing $P_1, P_2$ by their common refinement $P = P_1 \cup P_2$:<br>$$U(P,f,\alpha) - L(P,f,\alpha) \lt  2\varepsilon \implies f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$$<br>Furthermore, $U(P,f_j,\alpha) \lt  \int f_j d\alpha + \varepsilon$, so:<br>$$\int f d\alpha \le U(P,f,\alpha) \lt  \int f_1 d\alpha + \int f_2 d\alpha + 2\varepsilon \implies \int f d\alpha \le \int f_1 d\alpha + \int f_2 d\alpha$$<br>Replacing $f_1, f_2$ by $-f_1, -f_2$ reverses the inequality $\implies \int (f_1+f_2) d\alpha = \int f_1 d\alpha + \int f_2 d\alpha$.<br><br><em>Observación:</em> ¡Las demostraciones de las partes (b), (d) y (e) quedan como ejercicio para el lector! lol

CARD 2 "Theorem 6.12(c): Additivity over Subintervals [a,c] and [c,b]"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.12(c):</strong> Demostrar que si $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ en $[a,b]$ y $a \lt  c \lt  b$, entonces $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ en $[a,c]$ y en $[c,b]$, y:<br>$$\int_a^c f d\alpha + \int_c^b f d\alpha = \int_a^b f d\alpha$$
BACK
Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, choose a partition $P^*$ of $[a,b]$ such that $U(P^*,f,\alpha) - L(P^*,f,\alpha) \lt  \varepsilon$.<br><br>Form the refinement $P = P^* \cup \{c\}$ by adding the point $c$. By refinement properties:<br>$$U(P,f,\alpha) - L(P,f,\alpha) \le U(P^*,f,\alpha) - L(P^*,f,\alpha) \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>The partition $P$ splits into $P_1 = P \cap [a,c]$ (partition of $[a,c]$) and $P_2 = P \cap [c,b]$ (partition of $[c,b]$).<br><br>Since $U(P,f,\alpha) = U(P_1,f,\alpha) + U(P_2,f,\alpha)$ and $L(P,f,\alpha) = L(P_1,f,\alpha) + L(P_2,f,\alpha)$:<br>$$[U(P_1,f,\alpha) - L(P_1,f,\alpha)] + [U(P_2,f,\alpha) - L(P_2,f,\alpha)] = U(P,f,\alpha) - L(P,f,\alpha) \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>Since both terms are non-negative, each is $\lt  \varepsilon$, proving $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ on $[a,c]$ and $[c,b]$.<br><br>Furthermore:<br>$$\left| \int_a^c f d\alpha + \int_c^b f d\alpha - \int_a^b f d\alpha \right| \le U(P_1,f,\alpha) + U(P_2,f,\alpha) - L(P,f,\alpha) \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>Since $\varepsilon$ is arbitrary, equality $\int_a^c f d\alpha + \int_c^b f d\alpha = \int_a^b f d\alpha$ follows.

CARD 3 "Theorem 6.13: Products & Absolute Values of Integrable Functions"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.13:</strong> Si $f, g \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ en $[a,b]$, demostrar que:<br>(a) $fg \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$<br>(b) $|f| \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ y $\left|\int_a^b f d\alpha\right| \le \int_a^b |f| d\alpha$
BACK
<strong>Demostración de (a):</strong><br>Taking $\phi(t) = t^2$, Theorem 6.11 shows $f^2 \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$ whenever $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$.<br>Use the algebraic identity:<br>$$4fg = (f+g)^2 - (f-g)^2$$<br>Since $f+g$ and $f-g$ are in $\mathscr R(\alpha)$, their squares $(f+g)^2$ and $(f-g)^2$ are in $\mathscr R(\alpha)$. By linearity, $4fg \in \mathscr R(\alpha) \implies fg \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$.<br><br><strong>Demostración de (b):</strong><br>Taking $\phi(t) = |t|$, Theorem 6.11 shows $|f| \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$.<br>Choose $c = \pm 1$ so that $c \int_a^b f d\alpha \ge 0$. Since $c f(x) \le |f(x)|$ for all $x \in [a,b]$:<br>$$\left|\int_a^b f d\alpha\right| = c \int_a^b f d\alpha = \int_a^b c f d\alpha \le \int_a^b |f| d\alpha$$

CARD 4 "Theorem 6.16: Integration with Respect to Point-Mass Series Integrator"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.16:</strong> Suppose $c_n \ge 0$, $\sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n$ converges, $\{s_n\}$ is a sequence of distinct points in $(a,b)$, $\alpha(x) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n I(x - s_n)$, and $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$. Demostrar que:<br>$$\int_a^b f d\alpha = \sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n f(s_n)$$
BACK
Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, choose $N$ such that $\sum_{N+1}^\infty c_n \lt  \varepsilon$.<br><br>Split $\alpha = \alpha_1 + \alpha_2$, where $\alpha_1(x) = \sum_{n=1}^N c_n I(x - s_n)$ and $\alpha_2(x) = \sum_{N+1}^\infty c_n I(x - s_n)$.<br><br>By Theorems 6.12 and 6.15 (finite point masses):<br>$$\int_a^b f d\alpha_1 = \sum_{n=1}^N c_n f(s_n)$$<br>Since $\alpha_2(b) - \alpha_2(a) = \sum_{N+1}^\infty c_n \lt  \varepsilon$, by Theorem 6.12(d):<br>$$\left|\int_a^b f d\alpha_2\right| \le M\varepsilon \quad \text{where } M = \sup |f(x)|$$<br>Using $\alpha = \alpha_1 + \alpha_2$ and linearity (Theorem 6.12e):<br>$$\left| \int_a^b f d\alpha - \sum_{n=1}^N c_n f(s_n) \right| = \left| \int_a^b f d\alpha_2 \right| \le M\varepsilon$$<br>Letting $N \to \infty$, we obtain $\int_a^b f d\alpha = \sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n f(s_n)$.

