CARD 1 "Theorem 6.17: Reduction to Riemann Integral — Step 1: Partition & MVT"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.17 (Reduction to Riemann Integral):</strong><br>Assume $\alpha$ increases monotonically, $\alpha' \in \mathscr R$ on $[a,b]$, and $f$ is bounded on $[a,b]$.<br>Prove that $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha) \iff f\alpha' \in \mathscr R$, and in that case $\int_a^b f d\alpha = \int_a^b f(x)\alpha'(x) dx$.<br><br>Paso 1: Aplicar el Criterio de Integrabilidad a $\alpha'$ y el Teorema del Valor Medio:
BACK
Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, apply Theorem 6.6 to $\alpha'$: there is a partition $P = \{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ of $[a,b]$ such that:<br>$$U(P, \alpha') - L(P, \alpha') \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>By the Mean Value Theorem applied to $\alpha$ on $[x_{i-1}, x_i]$, there exist points $t_i \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]$ such that:<br>$$\Delta\alpha_i = \alpha'(t_i) \Delta x_i \quad (i = 1, \dots, n)$$

CARD 2 "Theorem 6.17: Reduction to Riemann Integral — Step 2: Bounding Sums"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.17:</strong> Paso 2: Acotar la diferencia entre la suma de Riemann-Stieltjes y la suma de Riemann para $f\alpha'$:
BACK
If $s_i \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]$, then $\sum_{i=1}^n |\alpha'(s_i) - \alpha'(t_i)| \Delta x_i \lt  \varepsilon$ (by $U(P,\alpha') - L(P,\alpha') \lt  \varepsilon$).<br><br>Put $M = \sup |f(x)|$. Since $\sum_{i=1}^n f(s_i) \Delta\alpha_i = \sum_{i=1}^n f(s_i)\alpha'(t_i) \Delta x_i$, it follows that:<br>$$\left| \sum_{i=1}^n f(s_i) \Delta\alpha_i - \sum_{i=1}^n f(s_i)\alpha'(s_i) \Delta x_i \right| \le M\varepsilon$$<br>In particular, $\sum_{i=1}^n f(s_i) \Delta\alpha_i \le U(P, f\alpha') + M\varepsilon$ for all choices of $s_i$, so:<br>$$U(P, f, \alpha) \le U(P, f\alpha') + M\varepsilon$$

CARD 3 "Theorem 6.17: Reduction to Riemann Integral — Step 3: Reverse Inequality"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.17:</strong> Paso 3: Demostrar la desigualdad inversa y establecer $|U(P, f, \alpha) - U(P, f\alpha')| \le M\varepsilon$:
BACK
The same argument applied to the upper sum of $f\alpha'$ yields:<br>$$U(P, f\alpha') \le U(P, f, \alpha) + M\varepsilon$$<br>Combining both inequalities gives:<br>$$|U(P, f, \alpha) - U(P, f\alpha')| \le M\varepsilon$$

CARD 4 "Theorem 6.17: Reduction to Riemann Integral — Step 4: Upper/Lower Integrals Equality"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.17:</strong> Paso 4: Extender a refinamientos, igualar las integrales superiores e inferiores y concluir el teorema:
BACK
Note that $U(P,\alpha') - L(P,\alpha') \lt  \varepsilon$ remains true if $P$ is replaced by any refinement, so $|U(P, f, \alpha) - U(P, f\alpha')| \le M\varepsilon$ holds for all refinements.<br><br>We conclude that:<br>$$\left| \overline{\int}_a^b f d\alpha - \overline{\int}_a^b f(x)\alpha'(x) dx \right| \le M\varepsilon$$<br>Since $\varepsilon$ is arbitrary:<br>$$\overline{\int}_a^b f d\alpha = \overline{\int}_a^b f(x)\alpha'(x) dx$$<br>The equality of lower integrals follows in exactly the same way. Thus $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha) \iff f\alpha' \in \mathscr R$, and $\int_a^b f d\alpha = \int_a^b f(x)\alpha'(x) dx$.

CARD 5 "Theorem 6.19: Change of Variable — Step 1: Partition Bijection & Sums"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.19 (Change of Variable in Riemann-Stieltjes Integrals):</strong><br>Suppose $\varphi$ is a strictly increasing continuous function mapping $[A,B]$ onto $[a,b]$, $\alpha$ is monotonic increasing on $[a,b]$, $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$, $\beta(y) = \alpha(\varphi(y))$, $g(y) = f(\varphi(y))$.<br><br>Prove $g \in \mathscr R(\beta)$ and $\int_A^B g d\beta = \int_a^b f d\alpha$.<br>Paso 1: Correspondencia biyectiva entre particiones $P$ y $Q$ y las sumas superiores e inferiores:
BACK
To each partition $P = \{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ of $[a,b]$ corresponds a partition $Q = \{y_0, \dots, y_n\}$ of $[A,B]$ such that $x_i = \varphi(y_i)$.<br><br>Since $\varphi$ is a continuous strictly increasing bijection from $[A,B]$ onto $[a,b]$, all partitions of $[A,B]$ are obtained in this way.<br><br>The values taken by $f$ on $[x_{i-1}, x_i]$ are exactly the same as those taken by $g$ on $[y_{i-1}, y_i]$, and $\Delta\beta_i = \alpha(\varphi(y_i)) - \alpha(\varphi(y_{i-1})) = \alpha(x_i) - \alpha(x_{i-1}) = \Delta\alpha_i$.<br><br>Therefore:<br>$$U(Q, g, \beta) = U(P, f, \alpha), \quad L(Q, g, \beta) = L(P, f, \alpha)$$

CARD 6 "Theorem 6.19: Change of Variable — Step 2: Proof & Classic Formula"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.19:</strong> Paso 2: Concluir $\int_A^B g d\beta = \int_a^b f d\alpha$ y deducir la fórmula clásica del cambio de variable para integrales de Riemann:
BACK
Since $f \in \mathscr R(\alpha)$, $P$ can be chosen so that both $U(P, f, \alpha)$ and $L(P, f, \alpha)$ are arbitrarily close to $\int_a^b f d\alpha$.<br><br>Combined with $U(Q, g, \beta) = U(P, f, \alpha)$ and Theorem 6.6, this proves $g \in \mathscr R(\beta)$ and:<br>$$\int_A^B g d\beta = \int_a^b f d\alpha$$<br><hr><p><strong>Caso Especial (Cambio de variable de Riemann):</strong><br>Take $\alpha(x) = x \implies \beta = \varphi$. If $\varphi' \in \mathscr R$ on $[A,B]$, applying Theorem 6.17 to the left side yields:<br>$$\int_a^b f(x) dx = \int_A^B f(\varphi(y)) \varphi'(y) dy$$</p>

NEW NOTE "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus & Integration by Parts (Rudin 6.20 - 6.22)"
MODE serial
TAGS analisis, integracion, integracion_por_partes, rudin_ch6, tfc
RETENTION 0.85
POS 3000.0, 780.0

CARD 1 "Theorem 6.20: FTC Part I — Step 1: Uniform Continuity of F"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.20 (FTC Part I) — Step 1: Uniform Continuity:</strong><br>Let $f \in \mathscr R$ on $[a,b]$. For $a \le x \le b$, put $F(x) = \int_a^x f(t) dt$.<br>Demostrar que $F$ es uniformemente continua en $[a,b]$:
BACK
Since $f \in \mathscr R$, $f$ is bounded. Suppose $|f(t)| \le M$ for $a \le t \le b$.<br><br>If $a \le x \lt  y \le b$, then by Theorem 6.12(c) and (d):<br>$$|F(y) - F(x)| = \left| \int_x^y f(t) dt \right| \le M(y - x)$$<br>Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, we see that $|F(y) - F(x)| \lt  \varepsilon$ provided that $|y - x| \lt  \varepsilon / M$.<br><br>This proves continuity (and in fact, uniform continuity) of $F$ on $[a,b]$.

CARD 2 "Theorem 6.20: FTC Part I — Step 2: Differentiability F'(x0) = f(x0)"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.20 (FTC Part I) — Step 2: Differentiability:</strong><br>Demostrar que si $f$ es continua en $x_0 \in [a,b]$, entonces $F$ es derivable en $x_0$ y $F'(x_0) = f(x_0)$:
BACK
Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, since $f$ is continuous at $x_0$, choose $\delta \gt  0$ such that $|f(t) - f(x_0)| \lt  \varepsilon$ if $|t - x_0| \lt  \delta$ and $a \le t \le b$.<br><br>Hence, if $x_0 - \delta \lt  s \le x_0 \le t \lt  x_0 + \delta$ and $a \le s \lt  t \le b$, we have by Theorem 6.12(d):<br>$$\left| \frac{F(t) - F(s)}{t - s} - f(x_0) \right| = \left| \frac{1}{t - s} \int_s^t [f(u) - f(x_0)] du \right| \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>It follows that $F'(x_0) = f(x_0)$.

CARD 3 "Theorem 6.21: The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (Part II)"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.21 (The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus — Part II):</strong><br>If $f \in \mathscr R$ on $[a,b]$ and if there is a differentiable function $F$ on $[a,b]$ such that $F' = f$, prove that:<br>$$\int_a^b f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$$
BACK
Given $\varepsilon \gt  0$, choose a partition $P = \{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ of $[a,b]$ so that $U(P,f) - L(P,f) \lt  \varepsilon$.<br><br>The Mean Value Theorem furnishes points $t_i \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]$ such that:<br>$$F(x_i) - F(x_{i-1}) = F'(t_i) \Delta x_i = f(t_i) \Delta x_i \quad (i = 1, \dots, n)$$<br>Summing over $i$:<br>$$\sum_{i=1}^n f(t_i) \Delta x_i = F(b) - F(a)$$<br>Since $\sum f(t_i) \Delta x_i$ and $\int_a^b f(x) dx$ both lie between $L(P,f)$ and $U(P,f)$, Theorem 6.7(c) yields:<br>$$\left| F(b) - F(a) - \int_a^b f(x) dx \right| \lt  \varepsilon$$<br>Since this holds for every $\varepsilon \gt  0$, the proof is complete: $\int_a^b f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)$.

CARD 4 "Theorem 6.22: Integration by Parts"
FRONT
<strong>Theorem 6.22 (Integration by Parts):</strong><br>Suppose $F$ and $G$ are differentiable functions on $[a,b]$, $F' = f \in \mathscr R$, and $G' = g \in \mathscr R$. Prove that:<br>$$\int_a^b F(x)g(x) dx = F(b)G(b) - F(a)G(a) - \int_a^b f(x)G(x) dx$$
BACK
Put $H(x) = F(x)G(x)$. By the product rule for derivatives, $H'(x) = F'(x)G(x) + F(x)G'(x) = f(x)G(x) + F(x)g(x)$.<br><br>By Theorem 6.13, products of integrable functions are integrable, so $H' \in \mathscr R$.<br><br>Apply Theorem 6.21 (FTC Part II) to $H$ and its derivative $H'$:<br>$$\int_a^b H'(x) dx = H(b) - H(a) \implies \int_a^b [f(x)G(x) + F(x)g(x)] dx = F(b)G(b) - F(a)G(a)$$<br>By linearity of the integral (Theorem 6.12a):<br>$$\int_a^b F(x)g(x) dx = F(b)G(b) - F(a)G(a) - \int_a^b f(x)G(x) dx$$

NEW NOTE "Serie de funciones."
MODE standard
RETENTION 0.85
POS 0.0, -520.0

CARD 1 "Series de funciones."
FRONT
Sean $(f_n)_n$ una secuencia de funciones tales que $\forall n \in \mathbb N.\ f_n:X \to V$ desde un espacio metrico a uno normado. Cual es la serie y cuando hay convergencia?
BACK
Definimos $S_n(x) = \sum_{i=1}^n f_i(x)$, entonces $S(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}S_n(x)$ si este converge.

